\subsection{Deployed reduced-MSE schedule and coefficient meaning}
\label{sec:riccati-similarity-design-guidance}
\label{sec:riccati-deployed-cadence}

The reduced physical-MSE calculation of
Sec.~\ref{sec:adaptation-reduced-mse-envelope}, together with the spectral
coefficient evaluation of Sec.~\ref{sec:adaptation-reduced-mse-validation},
sets the deployed OU--III regularizer target.  For the realized target cadence
\begin{equation}
T_{S,\star}=\clip(c_T\tau_\star,T_{S,\min},T_{S,\max}),
\label{eq:engineering-pseudo-cadence}
\end{equation}
the SpectralMSE target is
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
r_{S,\star}^{\mathrm{MSE}}
=C_J q_{\mathrm{eff}}^{1/14}
 \widehat\sigma_{a,B,\star}^{\,6/7}
 \tau_\star^{24/7}T_{S,\star}^{-1/2},
\qquad C_J=0.0538.}
\label{eq:adapt-default-rs-law}
\end{equation}
Here the acceleration amplitude is the physical band-limited wave statistic,
not the OU-prior standard deviation.  Under the strong-observation reduction
used to interpret the coefficient, $q_{\mathrm{eff}}\simeq2R_a h$ to leading
order.  The exponent $1/14$ makes the optimum only weakly sensitive to the
low-frequency acceleration-error intensity, whereas the $3/7$ dependence on
the displacement spectral moment in the preceding derivation gives the true
wave content substantially greater leverage over the optimum regularization
corner.

The dimensionless coefficient $C_J$ collects the normalized spectral moment of
the self-similar wave family and the numerical constants of the reduced
optimization.  It can therefore be estimated from the measured/reference
spectral shape rather than treated only as an unconstrained empirical gain.  The
record-wise spectral calculation gives the default $C_J=0.0538$ used by the
implementation; the full-filter study then tests, rather than defines, that
analytical operating point.

The factor $T_S^{-1/2}$ is already part of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-default-rs-law}; therefore the deployed SpectralMSE target
is \emph{not} multiplied again by the reference-cadence normalization derived
for the cubic construction.  Away from cadence clamps,
$T_S\propto\tau$ and
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
r_{S,\star}^{\mathrm{MSE}}
\propto\widehat\sigma_{a,B}^{\,6/7}\tau^{41/14}.}
\label{eq:adapt-mse-unclipped-scaling}
\end{equation}
Thus the deployed period exponent is $41/14\simeq2.929$ and the deployed
acceleration exponent is $6/7\simeq0.857$.

The resulting current schedule is
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\tau_\star&=c_\tau T_{\rm sea},\\
\sigma_{aw,\star}&=c_\sigma\widehat\sigma_{a,B},\\
r_{S,\star}&=C_J q_{\mathrm{eff}}^{1/14}
 \widehat\sigma_{a,B}^{\,6/7}\tau_\star^{24/7}T_{S,\star}^{-1/2}.
\end{aligned}}
\label{eq:adapt-three-layer-summary}
\end{equation}
The first two mappings set the approximate OU prior.  The third independently
sets the low-frequency regularizer by balancing drift rejection against
distortion of genuine displacement.

\subsection{Legacy dimensional cubic schedule}
\label{sec:adaptation-legacy-cubic}

Before the reduced physical-MSE optimum was available, the regularizer used the
dimensionally natural third-integral scale
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
r_{S,\star}^{\mathrm{legacy,base}}
=c_R\sigma_{aw,\star}\tau_\star^3.}
\label{eq:adapt-rs-base}
\end{equation}
The dimensions are correct because acceleration integrated three times has
units of $\mathrm{m\,s}$, the same as $S$.  This construction therefore gave a
portable dimensional base but did not by itself optimize the bias--distortion
tradeoff or determine its coefficient.

At the reference cadence $T_{S,0}$ the base can be interpreted as one
pseudo-measurement standard deviation.  Changing the update period while
preserving the same continuous-equivalent information rate requires
\begin{equation}
r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}^{\mathrm{legacy}}
=r_{S,\mathrm{appl}}^{\mathrm{legacy,base}}
\sqrt{\frac{T_{S,0}}{T_S}},
\label{eq:engineering-rs-filter}
\end{equation}
so, with $T_S\propto\tau$ away from clamps,
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}^{\mathrm{legacy}}
\propto\sigma_{aw}\tau^{5/2}.}
\label{eq:deployed-effective-law}
\end{equation}
The $5/2$ law is therefore the per-update realization of the dimensional cubic
base under a sea-scaled update cadence; it is not the present displacement-MSE
optimum.

For this historical comparator, combining the reduced corner relation with
Eqs.~\eqref{eq:adapt-rs-base} and \eqref{eq:engineering-rs-filter} gives
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
(\omega_R\tau)^6
=\frac{q_{\mathrm{eff}}}
 {c_R^2\sigma_{aw}^2T_{S,0}}.}
\label{eq:adapt-cR-spectral-meaning}
\end{equation}
This relation explains why the earlier amplitude-law experiments were
informative about fixed normalized pole placement even though their gain was
empirically tuned.

The cubic schedule remains a supported low-cost embedded option.  It requires
only ordinary products and the cadence square root already present in the
scheduler, whereas SpectralMSE evaluates fractional powers.  The paired study
shows a small but repeatable advantage for SpectralMSE over the calibrated
cubic comparator, so the reduced-MSE law is the default and the cubic law is a
computationally cheaper fallback rather than the analytical basis of the
current configuration.